- `.references/linux/net/mac80211/tx.c` — transmit sequencing.
- `.references/linux/include/linux/ieee80211.h` — 802.11 frame and IE
  definitions.

## Objective

Complete scanning/channel-dwell decisions, beacons, RSN, advertised rates,
authentication, association, BSS snapshots, timeouts and reconnection. **No
actual Wi-Fi scan or screenshot required.**

## Acceptance

- [ ] Implement/review the stated source and integrate on main.
- [ ] Reject malformed frames and never invent scan/association success.
- [ ] Ground decisions in public code, published standards and SDK/KDK
  references.
- [ ] Record unresolved assumptions and fail-closed behavior.
- [ ] Complete with source/static evidence, NEVER requiring a physical Dell
  test.

## Required source deliverables

- [ ] Connect received beacons/probe responses to bounded BSS snapshots,
      country-aware channel dwell, scan completion and native scan results.
      Active probe TX obeys the same calibration/regulatory gate as data TX.
- [ ] Implement BSS selection, authentication, association, RSN/rate/HT
      negotiation, required management responses and bounded state timers.
      Transition only on relevant validated frames/completions.
- [ ] Implement negotiated rates, aggregation/reordering and data framing,
      including malformed lengths, fragments, address/QoS interpretation,
      duplicate handling and queue-pressure limits at the real RX/TX boundary.
- [ ] Wire link loss, deauthentication, disconnect, reconnect and sleep/wake
      to key/queue invalidation and native status. Keep stale BSS/association
      events from changing a new connection generation.

## Start here and next implementation step

Frames.*, ScanCache.*, Station.*, LinkMonitor.* and WifiEngine.* provide
portable parsers/state. LegacyScanBridge has completed-scan snapshots; it is
not an Apple80211 dispatcher. EngineAction may borrow RX data: the native owner
must consume/copy it before recycling its DMA buffer and reject stale actions.

Connect bounded channel dwell to the radio/channel owner and connect real
beacon/probe RX and actual management TX completion to scan/auth/assoc timers.
Use one connection generation across station, keys, link monitor and queues.
Then implement negotiated rate control, aggregation/BA/reorder limits and
reconnect scheduling in the actual data path. Do not let configuration booleans
stand in for radio calibration or permission to transmit an active probe.

Behavior references: [mac80211
scan](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/net/mac80211/scan.c),
[station
management](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/master/net/mac80211/mlme.c),
[RX
aggregation](https://github.com/torvalds/linux)
and [TX
aggregation](https://github.com/torvalds/linux).
Consult protocol behavior without importing incompatible implementation code.

Useful regressions: hidden SSID after BSSID/channel/security changes, truncated
rates/RSN, failed auth/assoc retry, TX-buffer exhaustion before a state change,
beacon timeout across suspend, and stale events from a previous connection.

The portable `WifiEngine` now tracks a bounded 64-sequence receive history
per QoS TID plus a separate legacy data class, independently for
unicast and group-addressed frames on the currently selected association. Only
an already accepted, FCS-/DMA-validated and security-gated
non-EAPOL MSDU can advance the history. Retries, repeated sequence numbers,
and stale out-of-window frames do not emit a second native-delivery action;
small out-of-order gaps and 12-bit sequence wrapping have explicit behavior.
The history is cleared on candidate replacement, radio stop and engine reset.
Offline C++14/C++17 ASan/UBSan regressions cover duplicate/retry, rejected
protected frames, per-TID independence and 4095-to-0 rollover. This remains a
non-aggregating data path; negotiated BlockAck, A-MPDU reorder and actual
native RX delivery are still missing and must not be marked complete.

Portable link-loss admission now validates the complete optional IE suffix of
FCS-verified deauthentication and disassociation frames before revoking the
selected link. An otherwise credible AP reason code followed by a one-byte or
overlong element cannot trigger reconnect; complete optional vendor IEs remain
allowed. Synthetic C++14/C++17 sanitizer regressions reproduce the old
link-loss acceptance and preserve correct valid-frame processing. This does not
supply hardware management-frame integrity or a native reconnect pipeline.

